WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:03.509
One of the things you've heard me say many
times on the show, if you've been watching

00:00:03.509 --> 00:00:05.811
or listening for any period of time, is that
right?

00:00:05.811 --> 00:00:09.660
We are just genuinely really bad at predicting
recessions.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:13.349
Some people have been predicting a recession
for the last five years.

00:00:13.349 --> 00:00:16.840
Some have been saying it's six months away,
others it's 12 months away.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:21.000
Others, it's 18 months away and were really
just terrible at predicting when the next

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.390
recession is going to be.

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:27.810
But one of the things we do know is that we
are one day closer today than we were yesterday

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:29.280
to the next recession.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:30.990
That's just sort of a reality.

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:36.150
Unless you believe that something so fundamental
has changed that there will never be a recession

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:41.370
again, and if you're making that case well,
I, I'd love to hear why that's what you believe.

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:46.990
Number two, we do know that there are certain
signals that we can look at that will tell

00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:51.090
us whether we are imminently closer to a recession.

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:55.620
We're still not very good at predicting the
future state of the economy at any particular

00:00:55.620 --> 00:01:01.500
point in time, but we actually increasingly
have indicators that suggest we are getting

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:04.129
closer and closer to the next recession.

00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:07.740
The latest being that the yield curve has
inverted.

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:11.350
If you have no idea what that means, fear
not.

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:15.720
I am going to explain it to you hopefully
in terms that will make it clear to everybody

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:21.520
even if this is not in your educational or
or personal interest background, so don't

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:22.520
worry about that.

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:27.840
But before we get to that, some of the other
signals we've seen recently that are of concern,

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:32.400
there's one that doesn't get a lot of attention,
but it is important, which is that the number

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:39.170
of people, the percentage of people who are
90 days or more behind on car payments has

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:43.990
been going up, up, up, and this is really
important because it seems sort of innocuous

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:46.150
and as esoteric in some way.

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:48.470
90 plus days behind on car payments.

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:51.060
What does that tell us about the state of
the economy?

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:53.880
Car payments and car loans.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:02:01.689
Tell us a lot about the degree of financial
stress on people because to fall 30 days behind

00:02:01.689 --> 00:02:07.810
on a car payment because you had an unexpected
expense, uh, okay, to fall 60 days behind.

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:08.810
Okay.

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:14.030
But when people become 90 or more days behind
on car payments, cars that they presumably

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:18.850
need to get to work and earn money that they
need to get their kids to school or whatever

00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:24.770
else the case may be, that starts to be a
red flag about the personal financial situation

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:26.790
of many, many people in the United States.

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:28.090
That's been a red flag.

00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:35.250
Number two, the stock market eroticism as
a result of Donald Trump's trade war, including

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:40.480
basically market's being back down to where
we were a year and a half ago, does not bode

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:43.379
particularly well for the future of the economy.

00:02:43.379 --> 00:02:48.390
And then now yesterday, the yield curve has
inverted.

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:51.550
What does that mean and why do we care if
you don't understand it?

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:53.410
It's hard to see it as a red alert.

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:57.640
So let's try to dig into it so we can all
understand what I'm talking about in general

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:59.550
in the world of money.

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:06.610
In order to potentially earn a higher and
higher return, you either need to accept more

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:12.560
risk the risk of losing money or tie up your
money for longer periods of time.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:17.490
So if you look at cds, a certificate of deposit
you can get with a bank or financial institution.

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:21.830
If you're willing to lock your money up for
a year, you say, take my money, I can't touch

00:03:21.830 --> 00:03:25.940
it for a year, you might be offered a 2% return
on your CD.

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:30.470
These are hypothetical numbers by the way,
but if you're willing to say, I will lock

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:35.980
my money up for two years, you can in principle
earn a higher return.

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:41.379
Maybe it's a 3% return instead of a 2% return
because you're giving up access to the money

00:03:41.379 --> 00:03:43.530
for a longer period of time.

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:45.989
This is the way things typically work.

00:03:45.989 --> 00:03:51.860
Because if you were able to earn 2% on a one
year or a two year CD, most people would pick

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:55.030
the one year CD because it's a shorter lockup
period.

00:03:55.030 --> 00:04:00.450
So along the same lines, normally when you
look at the 10 year us treasury bond, the

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:07.680
interest rate yield on that is higher than
the yield on the two year us treasury bond.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:10.610
Basically for the same reasons that I just
discussed.

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:16.900
There's now been an inversion of the yield
curve where the 10 year us treasury bond briefly,

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:21.470
and this fluctuates all the time, had a lower
yield than the two year bond.

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:26.089
This is a marker with a very strong correlation
to recessions.

00:04:26.089 --> 00:04:31.289
If you look at the last seven recessions,
going back to 1969, all seven were proceeded

00:04:31.289 --> 00:04:36.490
by the 10 year bond yield, uh, dropping below
the two year bond yield.

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:41.819
And as you might remember, the Fed recently
lowered rates with the idea in part of keeping

00:04:41.819 --> 00:04:45.870
the 10 year bond yielding more than the two
year us treasury bond.

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:50.309
But the continued chaos with the Chinese trade
war that Donald Trump is waging and other

00:04:50.309 --> 00:04:52.550
things have been an opposing force.

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:56.750
And indeed the 10 year yield has dropped below
the two year yields.

00:04:56.750 --> 00:04:58.590
So why does this matter so much?

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:05.819
The 10 year treasury bond is considered a
very reliable, safe haven for investor money.

00:05:05.819 --> 00:05:11.810
The 10 year bond, if you believe in the general
future, stability of the economy is a very

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:13.949
safe place to put money.

00:05:13.949 --> 00:05:19.460
When the 10 year bond yields less than the
two year bond, it signals that there is a

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:24.699
very negative outlook about the future of
the economy in the next few years.

00:05:24.699 --> 00:05:25.699
Why?

00:05:25.699 --> 00:05:30.819
Because investors are so scared about the
two year outlook that they prefer to take

00:05:30.819 --> 00:05:38.449
the guaranteed return on the 10 year, even
if it's less to such a degree that it ends

00:05:38.449 --> 00:05:44.690
up a bidding down the yield because it is
seen as safer and more favorable for traders

00:05:44.690 --> 00:05:48.860
and for markets and for the economy, and it
effectively creates a feedback loop where

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:54.490
because of the lower yield banks become less
willing to lend money, which further causes

00:05:54.490 --> 00:05:59.409
an economic slow down and like much of this
stuff, these feedback loops go on and on and

00:05:59.409 --> 00:06:00.849
on and who knows where they stopped.

00:06:00.849 --> 00:06:02.219
So understand a few things.

00:06:02.219 --> 00:06:07.279
Number one, none of this changes that the
economy doesn't work for tens of millions

00:06:07.279 --> 00:06:08.279
of people.

00:06:08.279 --> 00:06:10.300
In the United States, maybe even hundreds
of millions.

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:11.300
That's understood.

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:12.509
I'm still a social democrat.

00:06:12.509 --> 00:06:15.569
I'm not jumping into finance bro mode or anything
like that.

00:06:15.569 --> 00:06:17.369
Uh, that's one thing to understand.

00:06:17.369 --> 00:06:20.750
Number two, we are always heading to the next
recession.

00:06:20.750 --> 00:06:26.520
The question is when and number three, increasingly
the indicators say that it's going to be soon

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:31.349
and we actually know of specific Trump policy
that's pushing us closer.

00:06:31.349 --> 00:06:34.550
Think back to 2012 before the 2012 election.

00:06:34.550 --> 00:06:38.770
Obama versus Romney, they both made claims
about the economy.

00:06:38.770 --> 00:06:44.149
The reality was that indicators, we're pretty
good at the time and the economy was likely

00:06:44.149 --> 00:06:48.819
to remain pretty good no matter who was elected
president in 2012 I said it at the time it

00:06:48.819 --> 00:06:50.159
remained true.

00:06:50.159 --> 00:06:57.009
We now actually have White House policy that
is specifically precipitating the next recession

00:06:57.009 --> 00:07:02.089
led by the disastrous trade war and we are
starting to see more and more indicators of

00:07:02.089 --> 00:07:03.089
that.

00:07:03.089 --> 00:07:04.639
I want to hear from you, let me know what
you think.

00:07:04.639 --> 00:07:06.469
Uh, and we will certainly do a followup and
continue to keep an eye on that yield curve.

